<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Shu</h1><p class="st-meta">934 CE – 965 CE · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second independent kingdom of Sichuan in the tenth century, a prosperous haven of poetry, painting and learning under the Meng family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Later Tang</a> conquered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-shu">Former Shu</a> in 925, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-zhixiang">Meng Zhixiang</a> was appointed military governor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>. When the Later Tang court tried to rein him in, he resisted, absorbed neighbouring eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> and in 934 proclaimed himself emperor of Shu, dying months later.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-chang">Meng Chang</a> reigned for over thirty years in peace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> prospered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu's</a> court became a cultural centre: the Huajian anthology of song lyrics was compiled there in 940, the bird and flower painter <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-huang">Huang</a> Quan worked in the palace, and the Confucian classics were carved in stone.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 964 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> invaded, and within weeks <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meng-chang">Meng Chang</a> surrendered in 965. He was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, where he died soon after. Heavy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> exactions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> later provoked rebellions there.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-shu.jpg" alt="Later Shu" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Later_Zhou.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>934 CE – 965 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Meng Zhixiang, Meng Chang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism, Daoism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shu (Meng Zhixiang), Meng Shu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=later-shu" aria-label="Later Shu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7376 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Shu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7696 1315L7898 1125M7696 1315L7901 1174"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7898 1125h0M7901 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chengdu. <a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=later-shu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
